ah","H2583":"to camp","H5921":"upon","H5892":"excitement","H1219":"to gather/restrain/fortify","H559":"to say","H1234":"to break up/open","H413":"to(wards)","H7200":"see","H2396":"Hezekiah","H3588":"for","H6440":"before","H4421":"battle","H3389":"Jerusalem","H3289":"to advise","H5973":"with","H8269":"ruler","H1368":"mighty man","H5640":"to close","H853":"[Obj.]","H4325":"water","H5869":"eye","H834":"which","H2351":"outside","H5826":"to help","H6908":"to gather","H5971":"people","H7227":"many","H3605":"all","H4599":"spring","H5158":"river","H7857":"to overflow","H8432":"midst","H776":"country/planet","H4100":"what?","H4672":"to find","H2388":"strengthen","H1129":"to build","H2346":"wall","H6555":"to break through","H5927":"rise","H4026":"(Hananel) Tower","H312":"another","H4407":"Millo","H1732":"David","H6213":"do","H7973":"missile","H7230":"abundance","H4043":"shield","H5414":"give","H7339":"street/plaza","H8179":"gate","H1696":"speak","H3824":"heart","H553":"to strengthen","H408":"not","H3372":"to fear","H2865":"to to be dismayed","H1995":"crowd","H2220":"arm","H1320":"flesh","H3068":"LORD","H430":"God","H3898":"to fight","H5564":"to support","H2088":"this","H7971":"depart","H5650":"servant/slave","H1931":"he/she/it","H3923":"Lachish","H4475":"dominion","H3541":"thus","H859":"you(m.s.)","H982":"to trust","H3427":"to dwell","H4692":"siege","H3808":"not","H5496":"to incite","H4191":"to die","H7458":"famine","H6772":"thirst","H5337":"to rescue","H3709":"palm","H5493":"remove","H1116":"high place","H4196":"altar","H259":"one","H7812":"to bow","H6999":"burn","H3045":"to know","H589":"I","H1":"father","H3201":"be able","H1471":"nation","H3027":"hand","H4310":"who?","H2763":"to devote/destroy","H6258":"now","H5377":"to deceive","H2063":"this","H539":"be faithful","H433":"god","H4467":"kingdom","H637":"also","H5750":"still","H5612":"document","H3789":"to write","H2778":"to taunt","H3478":"Israel","H3651":"right","H7121":"call to","H6963":"voice","H1419":"large","H3066":"Judahite","H926":"to dismay","H4616":"because","H3920":"to capture","H4639":"work","H120":"man","H6419":"to pray","H3470":"Isaiah","H1121":"child","H531":"Amoz","H5030":"prophet","H2199":"to cry out","H8064":"heaven","H4397":"messenger","H3582":"to hide","H2428":"Helech","H5057":"leader","H4264":"camp","H7725":"return","H1322":"shame","H1004":"place","H3329":"offspring","H4578":"belly","H8033":"there","H5307":"fall","H2719":"sword","H3467":"to save","H5095":"to guide","H5439":"around","H4503":"offering","H4030":"precious thing","H5375":"raise","H3117":"day","H1992":"they(masc.)","H2470":"weak","H5704":"till","H4159":"wonder","H1576":"recompense","H1361":"to exult","H3820":"heart","H1961":"to be","H7110":"wrath","H3665":"be humble","H1363":"height","H6239":"riches","H3519":"glory","H7235":"to multiply","H3966":"much","H214":"treasure","H3701":"money","H2091":"gold","H68":"stone","H3368":"precious","H1314":"spice","H3627":"article/utensil","H2532":"desire","H4543":"storage","H8393":"produce","H1715":"grain","H8492":"new wine","H3323":"oil","H723":"stall","H929":"animal","H5739":"flock","H220":"stall","H4735":"livestock","H6629":"flock","H1241":"cattle","H7399":"property","H4161":"exit","H1521":"Gihon","H5945":"high","H3474":"to smooth","H4295":"beneath","H4628":"west","H6743":"to rush","H3887":"to mock","H894":"Babylon","H1875":"to seek","H5800":"forsake","H5254":"to test","H3499":"remainder","H2617":"kindness","H2005":"look!","H2377":"vision","H7901":"lay down","H6912":"to bury","H4608":"ascent","H6913":"grave","H4194":"death","H4427":"to reign","H4519":"Manasseh","H8478":"under"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Truth, and sincere piety of the king, God was pleased to put him to trial; (Tirinus; Menochius; Du Hamel) or, “after these things were established.” (Junius) — Sennacherib attacked Juda when Ezechias had been fifteen years on the throne, and till that time had paid the stipulated tribute, 4 Kings xviii. 13. (Calmet) — He now probably refused to pay it any longer. (Salien, the year of the world 3319.) (Menochius)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Jerusalem; as many cities had fallen. The king entered into an agreement with the Assyrian, who soon broke it."],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","City. The torrent Cedron afforded the only good supply of water. It was often dry, and was only three steps across, when full of melted snow or rain. The waters of Siloe and Gihon were collected in it. Yet it was no very difficult enterprise for the king to turn the stream, and introduce the waters through the rock into a large reservoir, in the city, by the canal, which is mentioned [in] 2 Esdras ii. 14., and Ecclesiasticus xlviii. 19. The besieged were more frequently deprived of water, Judith vii. 6. Babylon was taken by Semiramis, Cyrus, and Alexander, after they had turned aside the waters of the Euphrates; and Cæsar obliged the town of Cahors to surrender, by intercepting the springs. (Frontin. iii. 7.) (Calmet)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Land, Cedron, on the east; (Calmet; Menochius) or it may be the Gihon, which supplies Siloe, ver. 30. (Tirinus)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Without, of less strength. — Mello, the palace, which was strongly situated, and Ezechias added fresh fortifications, 2 Kings v. 9. — Arms. Hebrew, “darts.”"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Gate, where there was a room for assemblies, 2 Kings xviii. 24. (Calmet) — Heart, in the most affectionate and pathetic manner. (Haydock)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","More: God and his angels, from whom he expected relief, and was not disappointed, 4 Kings vi. 16., and xix. 35. (Calmet)"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Lachis, whence he sent Rabsaces. Other blasphemous letters were dispatched from Lobna, (ver. 17) when Sennacherib was forced to go to meet Tharaca, 4 Kings xix. 9."],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","His altars. Rabsaces was ignorant of the law, and upbraids the king for a commendable action. (Calmet) — Thus our adversaries foolishly blame us for keeping holy days and abstinence. (Haydock)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","He (Sennacherib) wrote, when he was going towards Egypt. Both master and servant employed the same fallacious argument; as if the true God and idols were all upon a level, and as if past success were a sure proof of future victories, ver. 19. (Haydock)"],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Army. Hebrew, “camp.” — Disgrace. The Jews pretend that he was deprived of his beard and hair, (St. Jerome, Trad.) misapplying the text of Isaias vii. 20. (Calmet) — Sword, to prevent their being offered as victims, to appease the idol and the people. (R. Solomon) (Lyranus) — This happened some months after the king’s return to Ninive, when he had cruelly oppressed the captive Jews and Tobias, i. 24. (Tirinus)"],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","Many; probably strangers, 3 Kings viii. 41."],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Sign. See 4 Kings xx."],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Up, by riches, and the splendid embassy from Babylon. (Calmet) — Prosperity is more dangerous than adversity. (Worthington) — Few are able to bear good fortune with moderation. (Haydock)"],["haydock",27,null,"haydock","Arms. Hebrew, “shields.” Septuagint, “arsenals.”"],["haydock",30,null,"haydock","Underneath the walls of the city, making them run westward, chap. xxxiii. 14."],["haydock",31,null,"haydock","Wonder, at the defeat of Sennacherib; (Grotius; Du Hamel) or rather the retrogradation of the sun, as the Chaldeans studied astronomy with diligence. (Menochius) (Tirinus) (Calmet) — Left him, in punishment of his vanity. (Haydock) — Heart. How feeble is man when destitute of the divine assistance! (Menochius) — God permitted, therefore, the king to be tempted, (Calmet) that he might know himself, and that others might have a true idea of him. (Du Hamel)"],["haydock",33,null,"haydock","Above, for distinction’s sake: (Calmet) meriti prærogativa. (St. Jerome, Trad.) (Menochius) — The cell was either higher than the rest, or a sort of pyramid was erected over the tomb of Ezechias. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]},"fa":[[24,26,0,"The Twelve Books on the Institutes of the Coenobia","Book 11",0,435,"… \"But,\" it says, \"he did not render again according to the benefits which he had received, for his heart was lifted up; and wrath was kindled against him and against Judah and Jerusalem: and he humbled himself afterwards because his heart had been lifted up, both he and the inhabitants of Jerusalem, and therefore the wrath of the Lord came not upon them in the days of Hezekiah. How dangerous, how terrible is the malady of vanity! So much goodness, so many virtues, faith and devotion, great enough to prevail to change nature itself and the laws of the whole world, perish by a single act of pride! …"],[26,null,1,"The Christian Topography","Book 8",1,550,"… Wherefore, as he was again uplifted in mind by this conception of himself, he did not go after his victory into the temple, as was his duty, to render thanks and give glory to God, but he was uplifted with pride, as it is recorded in the second Book of Chronicles (xxxii, 26): That he humbled himself for the pride of his heart, and, God left him, to try him, that he might know all that was in his heart"],[31,null,1,"The Christian Topography","Book 8",1,550,"… Wherefore, as he was again uplifted in mind by this conception of himself, he did not go after his victory into the temple, as was his duty, to render thanks and give glory to God, but he was uplifted with pride, as it is recorded in the second Book of Chronicles (xxxii, 26): That he humbled himself for the pride of his heart, and, God left him, to try him, that he might know all that was in his heart"],[31,null,1,"The Christian Topography","Book 12",1,550,"But the Chaldaeans and the Medes and Persians, having a somewhat wider knowledge, were instructed by the building of the Tower, and the deluge, and by what happened in the case of Hezekiah and Jonah, and by the Captivity, and by Daniel and the Three Children, and also partly by the writings themselves. In like manner also the Egyptians were instructed by the affairs of Joseph and of Moses, and by the people of Israel, and these nations were thus better prepared for a ready acceptance of Christianity."]],"fau":[["cassian","John Cassian",0,"father",null,0,435],["cosmas-indicopleustes","Cosmas Indicopleustes",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,0]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 11 (1894)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf211"],["J. W. McCrindle, Hakluyt Society (1897)"]]}